Finite Element Method (FEM) remains the dominant approach for solving coupled thermo-mechanical problems. To enable practical implementation and computationally efficient interaction within the DT architecture, surrogate models, namely, reduced-order models and neural network approximators were integrated to replicate FEM accuracy with significantly lower computational time. In this work, the FE problem was formulated and solved using the ‘Weld Predictions’ software package. Thus, the components of the stress tensor were governed by the equilibrium equations for internal FEs and satisfy the prescribed boundary conditions on the surface ones. Correspondingly, the components of the nodal displacement increment vector Δ U i  = (Δ U ,Δ V ,Δ W ) were constrained by the associated displacement boundary conditions. The solution of the resulting system of linear algebraic equations for the nodal displacement increments at each integration step and iteration over Ψ was obtained by enforcing the principle of minimum potential energy, such that the functional  E I  is minimized, i.e. 9 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
