Specific parameters were grouped according to their dominant actuation bandwidth: (i) fast controls (arc power and wire-feed modulation) influencing melt-pool dynamics at millisecond-to-subsecond scales; (ii) medium controls (torch motion rate, micro-path adjustments and local orientation) operating at tens of milliseconds to seconds; and (iii) slow controls (inter-pass dwell time, global path replanning and preheating strategies) acting over seconds to minutes. Each group was governed by an independent controller optimizing objectives associated with its primary physical domain. A supervisory optimization layer coordinated these controllers through hierarchical multi-objective decision-making, prioritizing hard constraints related to safety and process stability while minimizing weighted deviations from geometric, thermal and productivity objectives. To coordinate the interaction between controllers operating at different bandwidths, the supervisory layer was formulated as a constrained multi-objective optimization problem. The overall objective function was expressed as 11 \documentclass[12pt]{minimal}
